<h2>The case in outline</h2>
<p>FIR No. 186/2025 of PS Sultanpuri began as a narcotics raid: 385.53 grams of heroin — an intermediate quantity — and 47.09 grams of Tramadol recovered on 10 March 2025. The investigation grew into an alleged syndicate case headed by one Kusum, and MCOCA was invoked. The appellant, Kusum's brother, was arrested on 26 October 2025. Nothing was recovered from him, and no act of procurement, sale or transport was attributed to him. The prosecution's case was financial: that he allowed his accounts, and his sons' accounts, to receive and move narcotics proceeds — about ₹2 crore in credits between 2022 and 2025, a ₹20 lakh transfer to Kusum, and payments towards agricultural land in Aligarh. The Special Court refused bail on 7 October 2026; the High Court heard his appeal under Section 12 MCOCA.</p>

<h2>What the court held</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Deposits are not proceeds:</strong> "The existence of substantial deposits cannot, however, be equated with the receipt of proceeds of organised crime." The prosecution must connect the money to the unlawful activity, not merely show that money moved.</li>
  <li><strong>Legitimate business explains the flow:</strong> the appellant ran two firms, and his income-tax return for AY 2024-25 showed gross business receipts of ₹34,73,300 — material that supported a lawful source for the banking activity.</li>
  <li><strong>Knowledge must appear independently:</strong> the court drew the line between an inadequately explained transaction and one knowingly undertaken as part of a syndicate's finances. At the bail stage, the records did not establish the unlawful origin of funds or the appellant's knowledge of it.</li>
  <li><strong>Parity considered, not mechanically applied:</strong> co-accused Anuradha and Deepa Singh had bail, and while those orders were "not conclusive", they remained relevant alongside the appellant's distinct role.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>The MCOCA framework at the bail stage</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>What the prosecution showed</h3>
    <p>Volume: large credits over three years, a transfer to the alleged syndicate head, property purchases, and a Section 18 confession. On a volume theory, every active bank account near a syndicate becomes suspect.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>What Section 21(4) demands</h3>
    <p>Linkage: independent material indicating that the specific funds originated in organised crime and that the account holder knew it. Admissibility of a confession at trial does not supply that linkage at the bail stage.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Why the judgment matters</h2>
<p>Financial-trail prosecutions are increasingly the backbone of organised crime and money-laundering cases, because bank records are easy to summon and hard to dispute. <em>Hariom</em> is a reminder that the records prove movement, not origin. For the defence, the judgment rewards early assembly of the innocent explanation: income-tax returns, GST filings, business registrations and invoices that account for the credits. For the prosecution, it signals that invoking MCOCA against a relative of the alleged kingpin requires role-specific material — layering patterns, admissions of knowledge, or transactions that make no commercial sense — rather than consanguinity plus turnover.</p>

<p>The court also restated two structural points of MCOCA practice. First, the absence of prior chargesheets against the individual does not take him out of the Act, because the continuing-unlawful-activity requirement attaches to the syndicate; what it does require is an independent assessment of his role. Second, a Section 18 confession is substantive evidence for trial, but it does not relieve the prosecution of showing, at the Section 21(4) stage, that the independent material sustains the charge.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Bail was granted on a personal bond of ₹50,000 with two sureties, monthly police reporting in the home district, surrender of passport and the usual non-interference conditions. The 103 days of unblemished interim bail did quiet work in the reasoning — in stringent-statute cases, scrupulous compliance with every interim condition is itself evidence for the final hearing.</p>
</div>
